analysis:
Both units are base-side excerpts of docs/threat-model.yaml, covering the tail of T-041 and entries T-033 through T-059. According to file_changes (range_map_complete=true), the PR changes:
- base lines 816-831, the TB-012 description and how_it_works (outside these units);
- base lines 2323-2327 and 2337, the T-059 mitigation and detection_hint;
- head lines 2365-2398, likely the new T-060 for add-on socket starvation and control-file read stalls;
- head lines 46-49, a header comment.

The patch excerpt shows TB-012 now records five items:
- the operator lifecycle mailbox (darkbloom addon) as another same-UID control channel;
- accept-time peer checks, possibly replacing the per-request LOCAL_PEERTOKEN checks;
- a socket path override;
- self-reported busy fields;
- a fail-open hold record.

The header also says T-059 adds a model-load fence.

These are documentation changes, not code. No visible line in these base units shows a regression. The changed head text for T-059 and T-060 is not visible here, so its accuracy cannot be validated.

Questions for integration:
1. Does the move from per-request to accept-time audit-token checks reflect actual code in AddonPeerPolicy.swift and AddonIPCServer.swift? Can a connection persist across a peer exec or a PID-reuse change? The base T-059 mitigation at line 2323 said per-request.
2. Does the socket override let a non-owner-only path, or a path outside ~/.darkbloom/runtime, be bound? That would weaken the 0600/0700 assumption.
3. What does fail-open on the hold record mean? A corrupt record could release a hold, letting models load beside running VMs (T-059, A-018).
4. Does the new T-060 correctly scope same-UID denial of service?

T-041 and the other entries in these units are unchanged.
